INT. CLIFFSIDE BARBERSHOP - DAY

A single chair bolted to a plank floor, facing a wide window that opens onto a canyon. The wind whistles through a crack in the frame. A mirror. A strop. A pot of lather.

JEDEDIAH CROWE, 60s, the barber, stands behind the chair in a clean white apron. Gentle hands. Soft voice. He hums as he whisks the lather.

COLTON MAYHEW, 30s, lies back in the chair with a towel across his throat. Dusty hat on his knee. Revolver on his hip, strap loose.

AGNES BELLWETHER, 50s, a widow in black, sits on a bench by the door with a sewing basket and does not look at either man.

JEDEDIAH
A little off the sides?

COLTON
Just the beard.

JEDEDIAH
Quiet morning.

COLTON
Suits me.

JEDEDIAH
Rode far?

COLTON
Far enough.

Jedediah strops the razor. The leather slaps. Agnes pricks her finger with the needle and doesn't flinch.

JEDEDIAH
Miss Agnes, you want coffee?

AGNES
No, thank you, Jed.

JEDEDIAH
Mr. Mayhew?

COLTON
Not before I'm shaved.

Jedediah smiles, spreads the lather, and begins to draw the razor along Colton's jaw. Slow. Deliberate. The canyon wind rises.

COLTON (CONT'D)
You know my name.

JEDEDIAH
Everybody does, since Tuesday.

Colton's eyes open.

COLTON
JEDEDIAH
(speaking at once)
What did you hear?
(speaking at once)
Not much. Just that you killed my son.

The razor rests at Colton's throat. Agnes sets down her sewing.

AGNES
Jed.

JEDEDIAH
It's all right, Agnes.

COLTON
I never--

JEDEDIAH
You never were sorry. I know. I read the report.

A very long moment. The wind drops. The only sound is Colton's heartbeat, loud in his throat.

JEDEDIAH (CONT'D)
But I'm a patient man, Mr. Mayhew. And Miss Agnes has a pie in the oven.

He lifts the razor away. Wipes it on the towel. Folds it shut.

JEDEDIAH (CONT'D)
The sheriff's on his way. He'll be here before the lather dries.

Colton lies perfectly still, as if one breath might cost him everything.
